<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Work the water path first, then the valve.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a Frigidaire refrigerator stops making ice, the fix is usually upstream of the ice maker. Check these in order: the water filter (replace every six months — a clogged one starves the maker), the supply valve and line behind the fridge, a frozen fill tube, the freezer temperature (it needs to be cold enough to cycle, around 0°F), and whether the ice maker is switched on with its feeler arm down. If water still dispenses fine but there's no ice, that's a Frigidaire tell — the dual-solenoid inlet valve has an ice side and a dispenser side that fail independently, and the ice side has gone. Filter and fill-tube fixes are yours to do; the inlet valve and module are a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>This page covers the ice maker built into your Frigidaire refrigerator. If yours is a standalone or undercounter ice machine, see the <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/">ice maker repair</a> hub instead. For fridge ice makers of any brand, the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ice-maker-issues/">refrigerator ice maker issues</a> guide is the umbrella.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 SAFETY / RECALL BLOCK -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Safety first — two Frigidaire recalls to check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Your ice bucket or level arm may be under recall.</h2>

    <p>Before you troubleshoot, it's worth a two-minute safety check. Frigidaire's parent company, Electrolux, has issued recalls where a plastic part inside the ice compartment can break into pieces that end up in the ice — a genuine choking and laceration hazard:</p>

    <ul class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>2022 recall — ice-level arm:</strong> roughly 370,000 refrigerators where the plastic ice-level (feeler) arm can break apart into the ice bucket.</li>
      <li><strong>February 2024 recall — slim ice bucket:</strong> about 400,000 side-by-side refrigerators (sold from late 2015 through 2019) with slim ice buckets that can crack and shed plastic pieces into the ice.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>If your Frigidaire is affected, the replacement part is <strong>free</strong>. Look up your model number on the official Electrolux recall site: <a href="https://www.ema-recall.com/icebucket/" rel="noopener nofollow">ema-recall.com/icebucket</a>. This is a manufacturer safety recall administered with the U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission — checking it is free and, if your unit is covered, the fix costs nothing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Frigidaire fridge ice maker stops</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, water-first.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Clogged water filter (most common)</h3>
      <p>A filter is good for about six months. Past that, flow drops and ice production tapers off and then stops — often with no other symptom. Replacing an overdue filter is the single most common fix.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Closed supply valve or kinked line</h3>
      <p>The water shutoff behind or under the fridge may be partly closed, and the line kinks when the fridge is pushed tight to the wall. No steady supply, no ice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Frozen fill tube</h3>
      <p>The small tube that fills the ice mold can freeze into a plug, usually from a slow dribble that refreezes. Thawing it clears the block; if it keeps refreezing, the valve or freezer temperature is the real cause.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Dual-solenoid inlet valve — the "water but no ice" fault</h3>
      <p>Frigidaire's inlet valve has separate solenoids for the dispenser and the ice maker. When the ice-side solenoid fails, water still dispenses but the mold never fills. It's confirmed with a meter and replaced — a tech job.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Warm freezer, ice maker off, or module failure</h3>
      <p>A freezer that isn't cold enough won't cycle the maker; an accidentally raised feeler arm or an off switch stops it entirely; and a failed ice maker module (motor, thermostat, heater) won't harvest. The first two are homeowner checks; the module is a tech replacement.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Run these before calling anyone.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Change the water filter</strong> if it's been more than six months. This alone restores a lot of Frigidaire ice makers.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the supply.</strong> Confirm the shutoff valve is fully open and the line behind the fridge isn't kinked.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm the ice maker is on and the arm is down.</strong> A raised feeler arm or an off switch stops production.</li>
      <li><strong>Verify the freezer temperature.</strong> It should be around 0°F. If the freezer is warm, fix that first.</li>
      <li><strong>Thaw a frozen fill tube.</strong> Warm it gently (a hair dryer on low or a warm cloth). Power-cycle the fridge afterward and give it several hours for the first batch.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> filter, supply, arm, temperature, fill-tube thaw, and a power-cycle reset are safe homeowner work and fix most no-ice calls. Do not replace the water inlet valve or open the ice maker module wiring — those are electrically live, plumbed-in tech jobs.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Water confirmed, still no ice? Now it's a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the filter is fresh, the supply is open, the freezer is cold, and there's still no ice, the fault is the dual-solenoid inlet valve or the ice maker module. Our techs verify the water path first, then test the ice-side solenoid and the module before quoting — so you replace the one part that actually failed. In LA's hard water, we also clear mineral scale off the inlet-valve screen and fill tube, which is a common no-cost flow fix. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>

    <p>More help: the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ice-maker-issues/">refrigerator ice maker issues</a> umbrella (all brands), the full <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/">refrigerator repair</a> hub, and if yours is a standalone unit, <a href="/services/ice-maker-repair/not-making-ice/">ice maker not making ice</a>. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/frigidaire-refrigerator-repair/">Frigidaire refrigerator repair</a> and <a href="/brands/frigidaire-ice-maker-repair/">Frigidaire ice maker repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/rancho-cucamonga/refrigerator-repair/">Rancho Cucamonga</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Frigidaire ice maker not working: common questions.</h2>

